  "live_records": {
    "q-149461": {
      "dom_id": "step-q149461",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "In summer, the daily maximum temperature in a particular city (rounded to the nearest degree) varies between ${25}^{∘}C$ and ${36}^{∘}C,$ inclusive. Assuming that each maximum temperature value has the same probability of occurring on a given summer day, what is the expected value of the daily maximum temperature?",
      "worked_solution": "Let $X$ represent the daily maximum temperature (rounded to the nearest degree) on a randomly selected day. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{25,26,…,36\\},$ and we wish to compute $\\text{E}[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U\\{a,a+1,…,b\\},$ then $X$ has the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{a+b}{2}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U\\{25,26,…,36\\},$ we have the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{25+36}{2}=30.5\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected value of the maximum temperature is ${30.5}^{∘}C.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
